<h1>Secure Hashing Checklist for Developers and Site Owners</h1>
<p>Hashing is simple to start and easy to misuse. A developer can calculate an MD5 or SHA-256 hash in one line, but choosing the right algorithm and context takes more care. This checklist gives site owners and developers a practical way to review hashing decisions across downloads, passwords, backups, APIs, and legacy systems.</p>
<p>First, identify the purpose. If the goal is file integrity, SHA-256 is usually a good default. If the goal is password storage, use Argon2id, bcrypt, scrypt, or PBKDF2 through a trusted library. If the goal is compatibility with an old MD5 value, document that limitation. Do not let one hash function become the answer to every problem.</p>
<p>Second, remove MD5 from new security-sensitive designs. MD5 may still appear for legacy checksums, but it should not protect passwords, signatures, certificates, or update trust. <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6151">RFC 6151</a> gives updated security considerations for MD5, and modern systems have better choices.</p>
<p>Third, use SHA-256 or stronger SHA-2 or SHA-3 options for new file checksums and release manifests. NIST's <a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/180-4/upd1/final">FIPS 180-4</a> covers the SHA-2 family, while NIST has also directed users away from SHA-1 for cryptographic protection. If you still publish SHA-1, plan a replacement.</p>
<p>Fourth, handle passwords separately. OWASP's <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html">Password Storage Cheat Sheet</a> recommends modern password hashing with salts and work factors. Do not use plain MD5, SHA-1, or SHA-256 for password databases. Do not write your own password hashing format unless you have expert review.</p>
<p>Fifth, store metadata. A stored password hash should make it clear which algorithm and parameters were used. A checksum manifest should include the algorithm name, file name, date, and source. Future maintainers should not need to guess what a value means.</p>
<p>Sixth, verify your workflows. Test checksum commands on Windows, macOS, and Linux if your users rely on all three. Test password rehashing during login. Test backup verification from restored files, not only from source files. A control that no one tests is easy to misunderstand.</p>
<p>Finally, assign ownership. Hashing decisions often sit between developers, operations, and security teams. Without an owner, weak algorithms remain because everyone assumes someone else will handle them. Put the review in a ticket, record the accepted algorithms, and set a date to review costs and standards again.</p>
<p>A short written policy helps. It can say SHA-256 for new checksums, Argon2id or bcrypt for passwords, no MD5 except documented legacy compatibility, and no SHA-1 for new cryptographic protection. This removes guesswork from routine development decisions.</p>
<p>Before putting this advice into production, write down the exact use case and risk level. File integrity, password storage, legacy compatibility, and message authentication are different problems. A clear note in the documentation prevents a future developer from copying the same hash choice into a place where it no longer fits.</p>
<p>The final point is maintenance. Hashing guidance changes as hardware, attacks, and standards change. Schedule a review once or twice a year. Replace weak algorithms, update password costs, retire unused legacy paths, and keep documentation clear. If this is your first review, start with <a href="/sha1-vs-md5-vs-sha256.html">SHA-1 vs MD5 vs SHA-256</a>, then check password systems with <a href="/password-hashing-vs-encryption.html">Password Hashing vs Encryption</a>.</p>
